#===============================================================================
# UNOS Liver Transplant Anti-Rejection Therapy Analysis
# TIME ORIGIN = DISCHARGE FROM THE INDEX ADMISSION
#
# Parses UNOS liver transplant patients into rejection-therapy groups
# (no rejection / steroid monotherapy / combined therapy / dual therapy),
# builds baseline recipient and donor tables, propensity-score-matches
# treatment arms, and runs landmark + time-varying survival and year-2
# outcome analyses.
#
# TO DO:
# 3.) "Survival curve" based on graft failure

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT CHANGED IN THIS VERSION, AND WHY
#
# The exposure landmark moved from 365.25 days to 90 days. That is a one-line
# change to a constant, but it is NOT a one-line change to the analysis,
# because several parts of the previous script defined treatment arms from
# year-long ID vectors that had no notion of a landmark at all. Left alone,
# the script would have carried two mutually inconsistent definitions of
# "Steroid group" -- one at day 90 (survival) and one over the full year
# (year-2 outcomes, demographic tables) -- and silently reported both.
#
# Structural changes made to prevent that:
#
#   1. ALL analysis parameters were hoisted into section 0b. Nothing
#      downstream hard-codes 365.25, 730.5, or 90 any more.
#
#   2. The landmark arm assignment (pat_rej_first + assign_arm_at_landmark)
#      moved UP, from the survival section to section 7, so that every
#      downstream section -- demographic tables, propensity matching,
#      survival, year-2 outcomes -- draws its arms from the same object.
#
#   3. The Control definition changed. See section 7; this is the one edit
#      that moves numbers rather than tidying them.
#
#   4. The year-2 outcome sections now take their arms from the landmark
#      assignment instead of the section-6 full-year ID vectors. The grouping
#      column was renamed FIRST_YEAR_GROUP -> EXPOSURE_GROUP, because at a
#      90-day landmark it is no longer a first-year group and leaving the old
#      name invites exactly the wrong sentence in a manuscript.
#
#   5. The appended add-on blocks (donor tables, timing histograms) were
#      folded into the main flow in dependency order rather than left at the
#      end as replacement patches.
#
# READ BEFORE INTERPRETING ANYTHING BELOW.
#
# THE EXPOSURE IS ANTI-REJECTION THERAPY DURING THE INDEX ADMISSION. Every
# exposure event in this cohort is a discharge record; there is not one dated
# outpatient rejection event before the landmark (section 18b confirms this,
# and it is why LANDMARK_DAY no longer affects arm membership at any value
# above INPATIENT_RX_DAY). Describe the exposure that way in the manuscript,
# not as "therapy within N days".
#
# THE TIME ORIGIN IS DISCHARGE. Section 19 is the primary survival analysis and
# section 19b the primary rejection outcome. Because the exposure is complete at
# discharge, anchoring there removes all the immortal time the design can 
# generate. It excludes deaths during the index admission rather than 
# deaths before day N, so it is not the less-exclusionary design -- it 
# excludes a different, larger, and more Control-weighted set.
#
# SENSITIVITY ANALYSES, same arms, different handling of time:
#   Section 20  landmark-anchored (discards deaths before LANDMARK_DAY; folds
#               post-landmark therapy into Control)
#   Section 21  time-varying exposure (no exclusions, no exposure window)
#   Section 21c side-by-side comparison of the anchors
#
# Sections 22-26 (year-2 binary outcomes) remain TRANSPLANT-anchored and are
# retained for comparability with the earlier version of this analysis. The
# discharge-anchored time-to-event version of the same question is section 19b,
# which is preferred: it uses the full follow-up rather than a fixed window and
# does not require conditioning on survival to day 365.
#===============================================================================


